<item>
  <title>Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors: The Directions That Stay on Track</title>
  <dc:creator>Jin Liu</dc:creator>
  <link>https://liu118-oss.github.io/posts/week-01/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 





<section id="a-complicated-name-for-a-visual-idea" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="a-complicated-name-for-a-visual-idea">A complicated name for a visual idea</h2>
<p>Imagine drawing arrows on a stretchy sheet. You pull the sheet so that the arrows change length and tilt. Most arrows now point along different lines. But a few special arrows may stay on their original lines, even as they stretch.</p>
<p>Those special arrows capture the idea of <strong>eigenvectors</strong>. Their <strong>eigenvalues</strong> tell us how much they are scaled.</p>
<p>The names sound abstract because we often meet them through an equation before seeing the question they answer: <strong>Are there directions in which a complicated transformation acts like simple multiplication?</strong></p>
</section>
<section id="think-of-a-matrix-as-a-rule-for-moving-arrows" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="think-of-a-matrix-as-a-rule-for-moving-arrows">Think of a matrix as a rule for moving arrows</h2>
<p>A vector is an arrow described by its coordinates. For example, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?(1,0)"> points one unit right, while <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?(1,1)"> points one unit right and one unit up.</p>
<p>A matrix can describe a <strong>linear transformation</strong>: a rule that preserves vector addition and multiplication by numbers. It can stretch, rotate, reflect, or shear arrows while keeping the origin fixed.</p>
<p>Consider this particular rule:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AA=%5Cbegin%7Bpmatrix%7D2&amp;1%5C%5C1&amp;2%5Cend%7Bpmatrix%7D,%0A%5Cqquad%0AA%5Cbegin%7Bpmatrix%7Dx%5C%5Cy%5Cend%7Bpmatrix%7D%0A=%5Cbegin%7Bpmatrix%7D2x+y%5C%5Cx+2y%5Cend%7Bpmatrix%7D.%0A"></p>
<p>The entries are instructions: double the first coordinate and add the second; then add the first coordinate to twice the second.</p>
<p>An arrow pointing right, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?(1,0)">, becomes <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?(2,1)">. It now tilts upward. The transformation changed both its length and its direction.</p>
<p>But try an arrow pointing diagonally northeast:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AA%5Cbegin%7Bpmatrix%7D1%5C%5C1%5Cend%7Bpmatrix%7D%0A=%5Cbegin%7Bpmatrix%7D3%5C%5C3%5Cend%7Bpmatrix%7D%0A=3%5Cbegin%7Bpmatrix%7D1%5C%5C1%5Cend%7Bpmatrix%7D.%0A"></p>
<p>This arrow gets three times as long but stays on the same line. Therefore, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?(1,1)"> is an eigenvector, with eigenvalue <strong>3</strong>.</p>
<p>Now try the other diagonal:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AA%5Cbegin%7Bpmatrix%7D1%5C%5C-1%5Cend%7Bpmatrix%7D%0A=%5Cbegin%7Bpmatrix%7D1%5C%5C-1%5Cend%7Bpmatrix%7D.%0A"></p>
<p>This arrow stays exactly where it was. It is also an eigenvector, with eigenvalue <strong>1</strong>.</p>
</section>
