<section id="the-equation-is-just-a-comparison" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-equation-is-just-a-comparison">The equation is just a comparison</h2>
<p>The formal definition is short:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AA%5Cmathbf%7Bv%7D=%5Clambda%5Cmathbf%7Bv%7D,%20%5Cqquad%20%5Cmathbf%7Bv%7D%5Cne%5Cmathbf%7B0%7D.%0A"></p>
<p>The left side says “apply the matrix.” The right side says “multiply the original vector by a single number.” An eigenvector is a nonzero vector for which those two operations give the same result. That number, <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%5Clambda"> (the Greek letter lambda), is the eigenvalue. <a href="https://math.mit.edu/~gs/linearalgebra/ila5/linearalgebra5_6-1.pdf">MIT’s introduction to eigenvalues and eigenvectors</a></p>
<p>The sign and size matter:</p>
<table class="caption-top table">
<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th>Eigenvalue</th>
<th>What happens to the eigenvector?</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?3"></td>
<td>Its length triples.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?1"></td>
<td>It stays unchanged.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?0.5"></td>
<td>Its length halves.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?-2"></td>
<td>Its length doubles and it points the opposite way.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?0"></td>
<td>It collapses to the zero vector.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>So “never changes direction” needs a qualification: a negative eigenvalue reverses the arrow, and zero removes its direction entirely. <strong>The transformed vector remains on the original line.</strong> Also, an eigenvector is not unique in length: <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?(2,2)"> works just as well as <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?(1,1)"> in our example.</p>
</section>
<section id="why-this-initially-feels-unintuitive" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="why-this-initially-feels-unintuitive">Why this initially feels unintuitive</h2>
<p>We usually describe this matrix using horizontal and vertical coordinates. From that viewpoint, it mixes the coordinates together. It seems natural to assume that mixing is unavoidable.</p>
<p>But our example becomes simpler when we use the two diagonal directions instead. Along one diagonal, the rule is “multiply by three.” Along the other, it is “multiply by one.”</p>
<p>Even the ordinary arrow can be broken into those two pieces:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0A%5Cbegin%7Bpmatrix%7D1%5C%5C0%5Cend%7Bpmatrix%7D%0A=%5Cfrac12%5Cbegin%7Bpmatrix%7D1%5C%5C1%5Cend%7Bpmatrix%7D%0A+%5Cfrac12%5Cbegin%7Bpmatrix%7D1%5C%5C-1%5Cend%7Bpmatrix%7D.%0A"></p>
<p>Apply the transformation repeatedly. After <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?n"> applications, the first piece has been multiplied by <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?3%5En">, while the second is unchanged:</p>
<p><img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?%0AA%5En%5Cbegin%7Bpmatrix%7D1%5C%5C0%5Cend%7Bpmatrix%7D%0A=%5Cfrac%7B3%5En%7D%7B2%7D%5Cbegin%7Bpmatrix%7D1%5C%5C1%5Cend%7Bpmatrix%7D%0A+%5Cfrac12%5Cbegin%7Bpmatrix%7D1%5C%5C-1%5Cend%7Bpmatrix%7D.%0A"></p>
<p>The first piece eventually dwarfs the second, so the resulting arrow points increasingly close to the northeast diagonal. The eigenvectors explain the patterns; the eigenvalues explain how those patterns evolve.</p>
<p>This convenient decomposition works here because the two eigenvectors span the plane. Not every matrix has enough independent eigenvectors for this approach. A 90-degree rotation in the real plane, for example, leaves no nonzero arrow on its original line, so it has no real eigenvectors. <a href="https://math.mit.edu/~roed/courses/211/lectures/Nov-8.pdf">MIT lecture notes</a></p>
</section>
<section id="an-economic-interpretation" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="an-economic-interpretation">An economic interpretation</h2>
<p>As a deliberately simplified model, imagine that the two coordinates represent deviations in activity in two sectors. Let our matrix describe how those deviations propagate in one period. These numbers are illustrative assumptions, not estimates from economic data.</p>
<p>The vector <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?(1,1)"> represents both sectors moving together. Under this rule, that common movement triples each period. The vector <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?(1,-1)"> represents one sector rising while the other falls by the same amount. That difference remains unchanged.</p>
<p>Instead of tracking two interacting coordinates, we can track <strong>a common movement and an opposing movement</strong>. In this toy model, common shocks amplify, while opposing shocks persist. Actual economic conclusions would depend on whether the chosen rule fits the economy, but the mathematical benefit is already visible.</p>
</section>
<section id="the-takeaway" class="level2">
<h2 class="anchored" data-anchor-id="the-takeaway">The takeaway</h2>
<p>Eigenvectors identify directions where a linear transformation reduces to scaling. Eigenvalues tell us the scale factor, including whether the arrow reverses or disappears.</p>
<p>The surprising part is that a rule that mixes coordinates can become simple when viewed along the right directions. The next time you see <img src="https://latex.codecogs.com/png.latex?A%5Cmathbf%7Bv%7D=%5Clambda%5Cmathbf%7Bv%7D">, picture an arrow staying on its line—and ask how much it stretches.</p>


</section>

